WEBVTT

00:00:00.381 --> 00:00:02.667
Last time we ended with two beakers that

00:00:02.687 --> 00:00:04.051
look exactly the same.

00:00:04.735 --> 00:00:07.210
One holds pure water, and one holds salt

00:00:07.272 --> 00:00:07.492
water.

00:00:08.281 --> 00:00:09.654
Now switch the hotplates on.

00:00:10.338 --> 00:00:12.744
The pure water heats up, starts to boil

00:00:12.825 --> 00:00:15.091
at a hundred degrees, and then stays at

00:00:15.132 --> 00:00:16.977
a hundred for as long as it boils.

00:00:17.781 --> 00:00:20.509
The salt water starts boiling a little higher,

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:21.893
at about a hundred and one,

00:00:22.415 --> 00:00:24.742
and as it boils, its temperature keeps creeping

00:00:24.863 --> 00:00:24.962
up.

00:00:25.868 --> 00:00:27.480
Same look, different behaviour.

00:00:28.468 --> 00:00:31.212
So what can a melting or boiling observation

00:00:31.314 --> 00:00:33.577
tell us about a sample, and what can't

00:00:33.619 --> 00:00:33.958
it tell us?

00:00:35.129 --> 00:00:37.136
Boiling point is a physical property.

00:00:37.719 --> 00:00:39.846
A physical property is something you can measure

00:00:40.028 --> 00:00:42.536
or observe without turning the substance into a

00:00:42.678 --> 00:00:43.500
different substance.

00:00:44.344 --> 00:00:46.849
Melting point is the temperature where a solid

00:00:46.951 --> 00:00:48.073
turns into a liquid.

00:00:48.635 --> 00:00:50.839
Boiling point is the temperature where a liquid

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:53.525
boils, forming gas all the way through it,

00:00:53.646 --> 00:00:54.688
not just at the surface.

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:59.660
Density, solubility and electrical conductivity are physical properties

00:00:59.721 --> 00:00:59.900
too.

00:01:00.944 --> 00:01:03.414
A pure substance has its own fixed values,

00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:05.161
which is why chemists can look them up

00:01:05.222 --> 00:01:05.965
in a data table.

00:01:06.582 --> 00:01:08.413
So why does the pure water stop at

00:01:08.453 --> 00:01:09.218
a hundred degrees?

00:01:10.102 --> 00:01:12.286
While it heats, the energy makes the water

00:01:12.327 --> 00:01:14.972
molecules move faster, so the temperature rises.

00:01:15.815 --> 00:01:18.404
Once it boils, the energy goes into pulling

00:01:18.485 --> 00:01:21.114
molecules away from their neighbours and out into

00:01:21.135 --> 00:01:21.536
the gas.

00:01:22.439 --> 00:01:24.519
Every molecule is the same, so they can

00:01:24.561 --> 00:01:26.040
all escape at the same temperature,

00:01:26.582 --> 00:01:28.802
and the temperature stays flat until all the

00:01:28.843 --> 00:01:29.643
liquid is gone.

00:01:30.506 --> 00:01:32.792
Plot temperature against time and you get a

00:01:32.873 --> 00:01:33.635
heating curve.

00:01:34.358 --> 00:01:36.706
A pure substance gives a flat section, called

00:01:36.727 --> 00:01:39.215
a plateau, at its melting point and at

00:01:39.236 --> 00:01:39.998
its boiling point.

00:01:40.868 --> 00:01:43.235
The salt water behaves differently, because it's a

00:01:43.296 --> 00:01:43.656
mixture.

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:46.247
Only the water molecules escape as gas.

00:01:46.871 --> 00:01:48.646
The ions from the salt stay behind.

00:01:49.312 --> 00:01:51.656
So as it boils, the water that's left

00:01:51.697 --> 00:01:52.458
gets saltier,

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.344
and saltier water needs a higher temperature to

00:01:55.405 --> 00:01:55.725
boil.

00:01:56.347 --> 00:01:58.634
That's why its temperature keeps creeping up instead

00:01:58.655 --> 00:01:59.397
of staying flat.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:03.048
Something similar happens when an impure solid melts.

00:02:03.711 --> 00:02:07.116
The different particles disrupt its regular arrangement, so

00:02:07.218 --> 00:02:09.100
it starts melting at a lower temperature,

00:02:09.642 --> 00:02:11.705
and it melts gradually, over a range.

00:02:12.549 --> 00:02:14.405
Here's how that shows up in data.

00:02:15.129 --> 00:02:18.056
Sample A melts sharply at three hundred and

00:02:18.137 --> 00:02:18.978
twenty seven degrees.

00:02:19.661 --> 00:02:21.247
All of it turns to liquid at that

00:02:21.428 --> 00:02:22.030
one temperature.

00:02:22.874 --> 00:02:25.818
Sample B starts to soften at sixty degrees,

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.944
and it isn't fully liquid until eighty, a

00:02:29.025 --> 00:02:30.106
twenty degree range.

00:02:31.049 --> 00:02:33.275
A sharp melting point is consistent with a

00:02:33.336 --> 00:02:34.178
pure substance.

00:02:34.821 --> 00:02:37.071
A melting range is physical evidence that a

00:02:37.132 --> 00:02:38.899
sample is probably a mixture.

00:02:39.704 --> 00:02:42.394
Candle wax behaves like sample B, because wax

00:02:42.556 --> 00:02:44.784
is a mixture of many similar compounds.

00:02:45.442 --> 00:02:47.159
But be careful with the sharp one.

00:02:47.703 --> 00:02:50.660
Consistent with pure isn't the same as proof

00:02:50.741 --> 00:02:51.123
of pure.

00:02:51.844 --> 00:02:54.326
A few mixtures boil at one steady temperature,

00:02:54.706 --> 00:02:56.287
just like a pure substance,

00:02:56.847 --> 00:02:59.069
and you'll meet one when we distil ethanol.

00:02:59.871 --> 00:03:02.118
Boiling points also change with air pressure.

00:03:02.701 --> 00:03:05.349
High on a mountain, pure water boils below

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:06.172
a hundred degrees.

00:03:06.896 --> 00:03:09.069
And one melting point can match more than

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:09.833
one substance.

00:03:10.536 --> 00:03:13.197
So chemists state the pressure, and they check

00:03:13.238 --> 00:03:16.539
at least two independent properties before they conclude

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:17.060
anything.

00:03:17.728 --> 00:03:18.513
Here's how that works.

00:03:19.219 --> 00:03:21.713
A white solid melts sharply at eight hundred

00:03:21.734 --> 00:03:22.357
and one degrees.

00:03:22.922 --> 00:03:25.351
Its density is two point one six grams

00:03:25.432 --> 00:03:27.922
per cubic centimetre, and it dissolves in water.

00:03:28.646 --> 00:03:29.712
Now check a data table.

00:03:30.317 --> 00:03:32.365
Sodium chloride melts at eight hundred and one

00:03:32.386 --> 00:03:34.675
degrees and has a density of two point

00:03:34.736 --> 00:03:35.157
one six.

00:03:35.841 --> 00:03:37.383
Two independent properties match,

00:03:37.966 --> 00:03:40.491
and it dissolves, so it's very likely sodium

00:03:40.532 --> 00:03:40.952
chloride.

00:03:41.614 --> 00:03:43.982
That's not certain, but it's much stronger evidence

00:03:44.023 --> 00:03:44.745
than one number.

00:03:45.361 --> 00:03:46.909
Some solids don't melt at all.

00:03:47.593 --> 00:03:49.870
Dry ice is solid carbon dioxide.

00:03:50.474 --> 00:03:52.656
At normal air pressure, it turns straight from

00:03:52.696 --> 00:03:55.218
a solid into a gas at about minus

00:03:55.238 --> 00:03:57.620
seventy eight degrees, without ever becoming a liquid.

00:03:58.321 --> 00:04:00.317
That change is called sublimation.

00:04:01.023 --> 00:04:03.302
It isn't melting, because there's no liquid stage.

00:04:03.846 --> 00:04:05.889
But it's still a sharp change at one

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:06.430
temperature,

00:04:06.972 --> 00:04:09.958
and the particles are carbon dioxide molecules before

00:04:10.099 --> 00:04:10.519
and after.

00:04:11.149 --> 00:04:12.215
That last point matters.

00:04:12.719 --> 00:04:16.007
Melting, boiling and sublimation are physical changes.

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:19.131
The particles spread out or pack together, but

00:04:19.172 --> 00:04:21.773
they stay the same particles, so cooling can

00:04:21.814 --> 00:04:22.654
reverse the change.

00:04:23.396 --> 00:04:25.764
A chemical change makes a new substance.

00:04:26.387 --> 00:04:28.571
Burn a strip of magnesium, and the shiny

00:04:28.612 --> 00:04:31.778
metal becomes a white powder, magnesium oxide, with

00:04:31.819 --> 00:04:32.861
completely different properties.

00:04:33.784 --> 00:04:36.149
So a change of state alone is never

00:04:36.291 --> 00:04:37.793
evidence of a chemical reaction.

00:04:38.196 --> 00:04:40.024
You need evidence of a new substance.

00:04:40.758 --> 00:04:42.071
Here's a way to keep it straight.

00:04:42.696 --> 00:04:46.186
Flat hints pure, a slope hints mixture, then

00:04:46.267 --> 00:04:47.029
check another property.

00:04:47.872 --> 00:04:50.417
A pure substance holds one temperature while it

00:04:50.458 --> 00:04:52.722
changes state, so its curve goes flat.

00:04:53.445 --> 00:04:55.686
A mixture's composition changes as it melts or

00:04:55.727 --> 00:04:57.368
boils, so its curve slopes.

00:04:58.391 --> 00:05:00.864
And because a hint isn't proof, you confirm

00:05:00.966 --> 00:05:01.991
with a second property.

00:05:04.724 --> 00:05:05.189
Your turn.

00:05:05.874 --> 00:05:08.250
Two white solids are heated slowly.

00:05:08.933 --> 00:05:11.235
Solid X melts completely at one hundred and

00:05:11.295 --> 00:05:14.098
twenty two degrees, and its temperature stays constant

00:05:14.198 --> 00:05:14.798
while it melts.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:18.107
Solid Y begins to melt at seventy degrees

00:05:18.448 --> 00:05:20.253
and is fully liquid at eighty four.

00:05:20.935 --> 00:05:22.419
Which is more likely to be a mixture,

00:05:23.101 --> 00:05:25.026
and is it right to say X must

00:05:25.086 --> 00:05:25.547
be pure?

00:05:26.433 --> 00:05:27.158
Pause here to answer.

00:05:37.823 --> 00:05:40.020
Solid Y is more likely a mixture.

00:05:40.523 --> 00:05:42.735
It melts over a fourteen degree range, from

00:05:42.815 --> 00:05:43.841
seventy to eighty four.

00:05:44.785 --> 00:05:47.592
Solid X's sharp melting point is consistent with

00:05:47.632 --> 00:05:48.574
a pure substance,

00:05:49.075 --> 00:05:51.381
but on its own it doesn't prove it.

00:05:52.083 --> 00:05:54.108
Compare it with data, and check a second

00:05:54.148 --> 00:05:56.575
property, such as density or solubility.

00:05:57.399 --> 00:05:59.429
Writing that X is definitely pure

00:05:59.933 --> 00:06:00.958
is the slip to avoid.

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:03.012
Last one.

00:06:03.858 --> 00:06:06.700
At normal air pressure, a white solid turns

00:06:06.961 --> 00:06:09.722
straight into a gas at about minus seventy

00:06:09.743 --> 00:06:10.283
eight degrees.

00:06:11.106 --> 00:06:13.178
When the gas is cooled, the same white

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:14.265
solid forms again.

00:06:15.214 --> 00:06:16.708
Name this change of state.

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:20.192
Then evaluate a student's claim that a chemical

00:06:20.233 --> 00:06:22.454
reaction happened, because a gas was produced.

00:06:23.118 --> 00:06:23.924
Pause here to answer.

00:06:34.528 --> 00:06:37.877
It's sublimation, a solid turning straight into a

00:06:37.918 --> 00:06:38.198
gas.

00:06:39.021 --> 00:06:39.848
And the claim is wrong.

00:06:40.553 --> 00:06:42.954
The same white solid forms again on cooling,

00:06:43.536 --> 00:06:45.297
so no new substance was made.

00:06:46.078 --> 00:06:48.039
The particles are the same before and after,

00:06:48.441 --> 00:06:49.701
so it's a physical change.

00:06:50.504 --> 00:06:52.800
Producing a gas isn't evidence of a reaction

00:06:52.841 --> 00:06:53.303
by itself.

00:06:54.962 --> 00:06:57.339
So properties help us identify a substance.

00:06:57.923 --> 00:06:59.540
They can also help us separate one.

00:07:00.285 --> 00:07:03.028
Here's a beaker of muddy, salty water, with

00:07:03.108 --> 00:07:05.110
sand, salt and water all together.

00:07:05.833 --> 00:07:08.190
The sand doesn't dissolve, but the salt does.

00:07:09.175 --> 00:07:11.097
Next time, we'll use that difference to get

00:07:11.137 --> 00:07:13.419
dry sand and dry salt back out of

00:07:13.559 --> 00:07:14.060
one mixture.

00:07:16.795 --> 00:07:17.617
Here are the key notes.

00:07:18.141 --> 00:07:19.688
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:19.729 --> 00:07:20.150
to copy them.
